    {
      "number": 2,
      "title": "The Adjugate Formula: A⁻¹ = adj(A) / det(A)",
      "abbr": "ECE",
      "subtopics": [
        {
          "number": "2.1",
          "title": "The Adjugate Formula: A⁻¹ = adj(A) / det(A)",
          "abbr": "ECE",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<p>The adjugate (also called adjoint) of a matrix is the transpose of its cofactor matrix. Each entry in the cofactor matrix is the cofactor Cᵢⱼ = (−1)ⁱ⁺╩▓ × Mᵢⱼ where Mᵢⱼ is the minor (determinant of the submatrix with row i and column j removed).</p>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<p>Once you have the cofactor matrix, you transpose it to get adj(A), then divide by det(A) to get A⁻¹. This method is used in the notes to find C⁻¹ via Cayley-Hamilton (Week 11), but it's also a standalone method.</p>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<strong>Adjugate Method</strong>"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "<div>A⁻¹ = adj(A) / det(A) where adj(A) = transpose of the cofactor matrix C Cᵢⱼ = (−1)ⁱ⁺╩▓ · Mᵢⱼ</div>"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For 2×2: adj([[a,b],[c,d]]) = [[d,−b],[−c,a]] | det = ad−bc | A⁻¹ = (1/(ad−bc))·[[d,−b],[−c,a]]"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<span>Find inverse of A = [[1,2],[2,3]] using adjugate formula</span>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<strong>01</strong>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<strong>Compute det(A)</strong>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<pre>A = [ 1 2 ] [ 2 3 ] det(A) = (1)(3) - (2)(2) = 3 - 4 = -1</pre>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<strong>02</strong>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<strong>Write adj(A) using 2×2 formula: swap diagonal, negate off-diagonal</strong>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<pre>adj(A) = [ 3 -2 ] ← swap a and d; negate b and c [ -2 1 ]</pre>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<strong>03</strong>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<strong>Compute A⁻¹ = adj(A) / det(A)</strong>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<pre>A⁻¹ = (1/-1) × [ 3 -2 ] = [ -3 2 ] [ -2 1 ] [ 2 -1 ]</pre>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<strong>04</strong>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<strong>Verify: A × A⁻¹ should equal I</strong>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<pre>[ 1 2 ] × [ -3 2 ] = [ 1(-3)+2(2) 1(2)+2(-1) ] [ 2 3 ] [ 2 -1 ] [ 2(-3)+3(2) 2(2)+3(-1) ] = [ -3+4 2-2 ] = [ 1 0 ] = I ✓ [ -6+6 4-3 ] [ 0 1 ]</pre>"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "<div>A⁻¹ = [[−3,2],[2,−1]] ✓ verified</div>"
            },
            {
              "type": "insight",
              "text": "Common mistake — In the 2×2 adjugate formula, students swap the diagonal entries but then also swap instead of negate the off-diagonal entries. The rule is: swap a₁₁ and a₂₂, then put a negative sign on a₁₂ and a₂₁. Do not swap them — negate them."
            }
          ],
